  data:  # 4c37
    kind: invariant  # 4c37
    title: "the file-presence pre-filter skips passes whose analyzer reads extensions the taxonomy does not know, recording no_candidate_files for a pass that never looked"  # 4c37
    status: violated  # 4c37
    priority: 2  # 4c37
    parent:  # 4c37
    tags:  # 4c37
    - analysis_quality  # 4c37
    - discovery  # 4c37
    - analyzer  # 4c37
    isbefore: []  # 4c37
    duplicate_of: []  # 4c37
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4c37
    pr_ref:  # 4c37
    description: "MEASURED 2026-09-17 at 2035bcc935 on ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/aardvark-dns, two\narms of the real dispatcher (the pre-filter monkeypatched to a no-op for arm B):\n\n    symbols: prefilter ON 359    OFF 367    delta 8\n\nTurning the file-presence pre-filter OFF finds MORE symbols. The pass is `ini`\nand the file is test/dnsmasq.conf.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `_filter_by_file_presence` short-circuits an analyzer when the\nPROFILE reports zero files for every language it declares. The profile counts\nfiles by globbing the ADR-0004 taxonomy's extension list. So the pre-filter is\nsound only when the taxonomy's patterns are a SUPERSET of what the analyzer\nactually reads. They had drifted:\n\n    taxonomy  ini : ['*.ini', '*.cfg']\n    analyzer  ini : ['*.ini','*.cfg','*.conf','.editorconfig','.flake8','.pylintrc']\n\nZero `.ini`/`.cfg` files => profile says 0 => pass short-circuited => the\n`.conf` file is never analysed.\n\nWHY IT IS WORSE THAN A MISSED FILE. The skip is recorded as\n`no_candidate_files`, which reads as \"ran and correctly found nothing\" when it\nmeans \"never looked\". That is the project's ABSENT != EMPTY defect wearing the\ncostume of the very axis built to prevent it, and no consumer can tell the two\napart from the artifact.\n\nTHE CLASS, ENUMERATED (6 of 118 analyzers; the other 4 flagged by the gate are\n`**/*.x` vs `*.x`, the same file set):\n\n    ini         also reads *.conf .editorconfig .flake8 .pylintrc\n    objc        also reads *.h\n    cpp         also reads *.h\n    fortran     also reads *.F03 *.F08 *.F95 *.fpp\n    javascript  also reads *.cts *.mts\n    verilog     also reads *.vh\n\nSo a repo of `.mts` modules gets JS/TS skipped; a Verilog project using `.vh`\nheaders gets zero symbols. Silent in every case.\n\nNOT the out-of-taxonomy population. An analyzer whose declared language is\nabsent from the taxonomy hits the pre-filter's own fail-open branch and is\ndispatched defensively, so it CANNOT be wrongly skipped. The damage is entirely\nin the IN-taxonomy set, where the pre-filter trusts a narrower list than the\nanalyzer reads.\n\nRELATION TO INV-hokig (satisfied). INV-hokig's statement is the invariant\nviolated here -- \"every layer that counts or enumerates files for a given\nlanguage must apply the same exclude policy; profile.languages[L].files and the\ncount of files analyzed by the language-L analyzer pass must agree\". It was\nfiled and closed on ONE instance (bash, exclude-policy divergence) and shipped\nthe cure: `@register_analyzer(..., find_files=...)`, which makes the profile\ncount with the analyzer's own enumeration. The cure was wired for exactly one\nanalyzer. This row is the same invariant reached by a different route\n(extension-set divergence) and closes the CLASS rather than an instance. Filed\nseparately rather than reopening, because the root cause differs and INV-hokig\nclosed on positive evidence for its own repro.\n\nFIX SHIPPED. `find_files=` wired on ini / cpp / objc / fortran / verilog --\neach already HAD the function, none passed it. `*.mts` / `*.cts` added to the\ntaxonomy's typescript entry instead, because the javascript analyzer declares\nfour languages and wiring its find_files would make `javascript`'s count\nswallow every `.ts` file and double-count against `typescript`.\n\n`*.h` is why this cannot be fixed by widening the taxonomy alone: it is claimed\nby `c`, and `_EXTENSION_MAP` is extension-to-ONE-language. `find_cpp_files`\nresolves it by content (\"`.h` is C++ only when .cpp/.cc/.cxx sources exist\"),\nwhich no static glob list can express.\n\nGATE. test_prefilter_candidate_set.py::test_no_analyzer_reads_files_its_taxonomy_entry_cannot_see\nasserts that any in-taxonomy analyzer whose `file_patterns` exceed its\ntaxonomy extensions declares `find_files`. Derive-or-gate rather than a second\nregistry to maintain.\n\nCLOSURE EVIDENCE. Re-ran the filed repro post-fix: prefilter ON 367, OFF 367,\ndelta 0.\n\nREFUSED, deliberately: adding a `make` -> `makefile` alias so the `make`\nanalyzer becomes pre-filterable. taxonomy `makefile` is ['Makefile','*.mk'];\nthe analyzer also reads 'makefile' (lowercase) and 'GNUmakefile'. Making it\npre-filterable would skip the pass on any repo using either -- manufacturing a\nfresh instance of this exact defect in the recall-loss direction.\n\nNOT CLAIMED: corpus-wide measurement. One repo for the behavioural repro; the\nclass enumeration is over the whole 118-analyzer catalogue and is exact."  # 4c37
    fields:  # 4c37
      statement: "An analyzer may be short-circuited by the file-presence pre-filter only when the profile's file count covers every pattern that analyzer would actually read; profile.languages[L].files must be a superset of the L-analyzer's candidate set, or the analyzer must supply find_files so the profile counts with its own enumeration."  # 4c37
      root_cause: "_filter_by_file_presence trusts profile.languages, which globs the ADR-0004 taxonomy extension list; six in-taxonomy analyzers declare file_patterns exceeding that list, so a repo containing only the un-modelled extensions yields a zero count and the pass is skipped as no_candidate_files."  # 4c37
